From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 5 Jul 2026 17:42:08 -0700
Subject: [PATCH net-next v1 00/11] net: flow_dissector: opt-in byte-identical fast paths for common shapes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

__skb_flow_dissect() parses packet headers with a generic protocol-graph
parser. This series adds an opt-in, static-branch-gated, straight-line
fast path for the packet shapes that dominate real deployments. Each
shape has its own gate under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/, every gate is
disabled by default, and when a gate is off the added cost is one
not-taken branch per dissect.

The series is byte-identical by contract: for any packet, the fast path
either writes exactly the flow_keys bytes the slow path would have
written, or it returns false and the slow path runs. That contract is
enforced in-tree by a KUnit equivalence suite (patch 10).

Patch 1 stands slightly apart: it is a small win for the existing path —
today every dissect takes rcu_read_lock() and does two dependent
rcu_dereference() loads to look for a netns BPF flow dissector program
even when none is attached anywhere; a static key (mirroring
bpf_sk_lookup_enabled) skips all of it in the common no-program case.

A follow-up RFC thread proposes descending into VXLAN/Geneve/GTP-U inner
headers (a behaviour change, posted separately for design discussion) and
an adaptive auto-enable controller built on the counters added here.

Performance summary
-------------------
Measured across 3 ISAs and 8 microarchitectures, with N=3 replicates
in-kernel and byte-identical verification per shape.

Reduction in dissector CPU cost (isolated per-shape microbench, all
fast-paths compiled in; range across the 7 measurable uarches):

      eth_ip    -4.7% .. -31.6%   (isolated eth+IPv4+TCP: -47% .. -55%)
      vlan     -18.1% .. -38.5%
      qinq     -16.1% .. -39.8%
      pppoe     -8.6% .. -28.0%
      mpls      -6.7% .. -15.0%
      ipip     -36.8% .. -47.0%   (largest; slow path re-parses inner IP)
      gre       byte-identical descent family (~ipip)

In-kernel, cleanly above the N=3 noise floor: eth_ip -112 cyc/pkt
(-4.9%) and vlan -148 cyc/pkt (-4.9%) on 25 GbE pairs. Consistent
across architectures; largest on in-order cores.

Isolated eth + IPv4 + TCP, userspace A/B (same source compiled two
ways), nanoseconds per packet, lower is better:

    x86 Zen 2  (Threadripper PRO 3945WX)   12.4 -> 6.6   -47%
    x86 Skylake (Core i9-10885H)           10.6 -> 5.6   -47%
    x86 Haswell (Celeron 2955U)            35.9 -> 18.0  -50%
    ARM Cortex-A53                        119.0 -> 61.3  -48%
    ARM Cortex-A72                         42.4 -> 19.1  -55%
    ARM Cortex-A76                         19.4 -> 9.3   -52%
    RISC-V SpacemiT X60                    98.2 -> 49.3  -50%

The fast path executes about half as many instructions per dissect,
consistent across three ISAs / eight microarchitectures; the relative
reduction is largest on in-order cores. IPv6 uses the same gate and is
byte-identical (x86 -39%, RISC-V -25%). One exception: on x86 Zen 1 the
base eth_ip reduction is not measurable at the median, though Zen 1 still
wins clearly on the more complex shapes.

The dissector's output is not a local optimisation detail. flow_keys ->
skb->hash is the canonical flow identity consumed by ~50 call sites:
ECMP/multipath route selection, RSS, RPS/RFS, bonding/LAG
(xmit_hash_policy), tc-flower, and hardware aRFS. Everything that spreads
or classifies a flow derives from it, so the per-dissect saving applies
to all of them at once.

Why not just optimise the existing loop
---------------------------------------
struct flow_dissector carries a per-instance used_keys bitmask and a
per-instance offset[] table, because custom dissector instances
(tc-flower) are built at runtime. Every key access in the generic walk is
therefore a runtime bit test plus an indirect offset load that the
compiler cannot constant-fold. The fast path's eligibility gate restricts
it to the two standard dissectors (flow_keys_dissector and
flow_keys_dissector_symmetric), which is exactly what turns those offsets
into compile-time constants and the parse into straight-line code — the
measured wins above come from that specialisation. Historical
optimisations of this file (load-width tweaks, the flow_keys_basic
smaller-memset rework, siphash key alignment) were each worth single-digit
percent on subsets; the specialised path is the first mechanism to reach
double digits per shape. Patch 1 contributes the remaining generic win we
found (the unconditional BPF run_array lookup).

Relationship to the BPF flow dissector
--------------------------------------
The fast path is invoked strictly after the netns BPF flow dissector
hook in __skb_flow_dissect(): if a program is attached and returns any
verdict other than BPF_FLOW_DISSECTOR_CONTINUE, the function returns
before flow_dissect_fast() is reached. Attached BPF dissectors therefore
always take precedence, and a system running one sees bit-for-bit
unchanged behaviour from this series (patch 1 additionally makes its
program lookup cheaper for everyone else).

The BPF dissector solves a different problem: it is per-netns operator
policy that requires writing, loading and maintaining a program, and the
reference implementation (tools/testing/selftests/bpf/progs/bpf_flow.c)
does not cover all shapes (no PPPoE, for example). This series improves
the in-kernel default that every system falls back to — opt-in per
shape, no userspace dependency. There is precedent for carrying a second
implementation of this logic: Documentation/bpf/prog_flow_dissector.rst
describes the BPF dissector as a reimplementation of the C dissector.
What that precedent lacked was an in-tree verification story tying the
implementations together; patch 10 provides one for this series.

Dissects fully handled by a BPF program are deliberately not counted in
/proc/net/flow_dissector_stats (patch 9): the file counts only dissects
that reach the in-kernel dissector, keeping fast_hits/dissects an
undiluted hit-rate for the code these gates control.

Maintenance burden: why two paths won't diverge
-----------------------------------------------
The duplicated parsing logic is ~960 lines of straight-line header
extraction with no state machine; the slow path remains the single
source of semantic truth and the fast path is verified against it, not
vice versa. Three layers enforce that:

  1. The eligibility gate: only the two standard dissectors and a flag
     subset ever enter flow_dissect_fast(); custom tc-flower dissector
     instances are structurally unaffected.
  2. Every shape is a default-off static branch: a kernel with gates off
     executes the not-taken-branch slow path only.
  3. The KUnit equivalence suite (patch 10) dissects a corpus of
     eligible shapes, deliberate fast-path misses, truncations at every
     byte boundary, and skb-mode entry conditions through both paths and
     memcmp()s the resulting struct flow_keys. Future divergence is a
     failing test, not a silent behaviour change.

The test earns its place: during development it caught two real
divergences the out-of-tree A/B harness had missed — the MPLS fast path
skipping the slow path's out_good terminal writes, and the 4in6 descent
not reproducing the outer-IPv6 residue the slow path leaves in the
flow_keys addrs union. Both are fixed in this posting; that is precisely
the class of subtle divergence the suite exists to catch.

Safety and the byte-identical contract
--------------------------------------
Patches 2-8 are byte-identical: the fast path either writes the same
flow_keys as the slow path, or it returns false and the slow path runs.
These are the only two outcomes, by construction, and patch 11 enforces
them in-tree.

Patch 9 (per-shape counters) does not change dissection at all — it adds
per-cpu counters and a read-only /proc/net/flow_dissector_stats: for
each shape, how many packets the slow path handled (occurrences), how
many the fast body handled (fast_hits), the resulting eligible fraction,
and the current gate state. Exactly one of the two is incremented per
shaped packet depending on gate state, so (occurrences + fast_hits) /
dissects is a gate-invariant measure of how much traffic each shape's
fast body *would* handle — the signal for whether a gate is worth
enabling. Cost is one this_cpu_inc on the already-hot path, summed only
on read.

That signal makes enablement decidable rather than guessed, because we
measured *both* halves of the per-packet cost model: the per-match
saving S (per shape per uarch) and the hot-path miss cost C (the penalty
a non-matching packet pays when a gate is on). Enabling a shape is
net-positive exactly when its matching fraction exceeds the break-even
p_be = C / (S + C) — a derived, measured number per shape per uarch (2-12%
for the mainstream byte-identical shapes on out-of-order cores, 8-19% on
in-order; MPLS is the high-bar exception).

Robustness and safety limits
----------------------------
The fast path descends into IP-in-IP and GRE tunnels by recursion. The
slow path bounds header descents at MAX_FLOW_DISSECT_HDRS; patch 10
gives the fast path the same bound (skb_flow_dissect_allowed semantics):
once the descended-header count exceeds the limit the helper returns
false and the dissect falls back to the slow path, which caps at the
same point. This bounds the recursion regardless of packet size (a
crafted deeply nested tunnel chain can otherwise drive one C stack
frame per ~20 bytes of linear header) and keeps the output identical to
the slow path past the cap. The audit that found this also confirmed the
header reads are safe for non-linear skbs (the fast path reads only the
linear head and defers to the slow path's __skb_header_pointer
otherwise), that the bounds checks are signed-correct, and that an
attached BPF program is consulted first. The KUnit suite covers the
tunnel-bound boundary, the non-linear-skb fallback, and adds a seeded
fuzzer as a general guard.

Testing
-------
This series has been tested across three ISAs and eight microarchitectures
(x86 Zen1/Zen2/Skylake/Haswell, ARM Cortex-A53/A72/A76, RISC-V X60), with
N=3 replicates on the in-kernel runs and byte-identical verification per
shape both in-tree (patch 11; run with
./tools/testing/kunit/kunit.py run --arch=x86_64 --kconfig_add CONFIG_NET=y
--kconfig_add CONFIG_FLOW_DISSECTOR_KUNIT_TEST=y flow_dissector_fastpath)
and out of tree. Methods:

  - the KUnit fast/slow equivalence suite (46 tests: shapes, misses,
    a truncation sweep, both eligible dissectors, a deep-nest
    tunnel-recursion bound check, a non-linear/frag skb, corner
    protocols, and a seeded fuzzer);
  - userspace A/B dissector microbench (isolated ns/pkt), per shape;
  - kernel-pktgen cycles/pkt via ksoftirqd perf-stat (the clean CPU signal);
  - iperf3 wire-saturated macro (receiver softirq % under RSS/RPS/RFS and
    tc-flower load);
  - 10-hour soaks; mixed real-traffic tcpreplay; PCAP miss-cost microbench
    (worst-case dispatcher fall-through); a flow_keys consumer/field audit;
    a 25 GbE line-rate characterisation; cross-uarch macro; a RISC-V
    dual-sender saturation stress; and a live WireGuard+bpftrace trace
    (__skb_flow_dissect ran 1.1M times in 10s on a single 902 Mbit/s flow).

Enabling
--------
Every gate defaults off; an unused gate is one not-taken branch. Enable
the shapes present in your traffic — the byte-identical gates (eth_ip,
vlan, qinq, pppoe, mpls, ipip, gre) are safe to turn on wherever that
shape appears. Each sysctl is documented in
Documentation/admin-guide/sysctl/net.rst.

Limitations
-----------
  - This is a per-packet CPU reduction. On a host limited by link rate,
    packet rate, or queue depth it appears as available CPU, not as
    higher throughput. A 25 GbE characterisation confirmed the MTU-1500
    ceiling on that hardware is a NIC packet-rate wall, not CPU.
  - Absolute nanosecond values are per-architecture baselines; the
    cross-architecture ratios are the comparison.

Supporting data (microbench harness and full per-shape/per-arch matrix,
perf-counter breakdowns, byte-identical checks, WireGuard trace):
https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2 , under perf-results/ .

